WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.906
Matrix multiplication's

00:00:01.718 --> 00:00:03.309
exponent is exactly

00:00:03.121 --> 00:00:04.170
nine fourths

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:05.865
A new OpenAI

00:00:05.677 --> 00:00:06.773
preprint proves a

00:00:06.585 --> 00:00:07.610
lower bound for

00:00:07.422 --> 00:00:08.589
multiplying square

00:00:08.401 --> 00:00:09.238
matrices

00:00:09.439 --> 00:00:10.771
any formal arithmetic

00:00:10.583 --> 00:00:11.549
circuit needs at

00:00:11.361 --> 00:00:12.186
least n to

00:00:11.998 --> 00:00:13.070
the nine fourths

00:00:13.142 --> 00:00:13.978
over two

00:00:14.168 --> 00:00:15.983
multiplication or division

00:00:15.795 --> 00:00:16.467
gates

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:18.027
The matching upper

00:00:17.839 --> 00:00:18.888
bound was already

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.398
known from OpenAI

00:00:20.209 --> 00:00:21.270
over the complex

00:00:21.082 --> 00:00:22.084
numbers and from

00:00:21.966 --> 00:00:22.638
Amanov

00:00:22.827 --> 00:00:23.935
van den Berg

00:00:23.877 --> 00:00:25.079
and Zuiddam over

00:00:25.032 --> 00:00:25.987
every field

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:27.606
Together they pin

00:00:27.418 --> 00:00:28.467
the exponent at

00:00:28.279 --> 00:00:30.201
exactly 2.25

00:00:30.295 --> 00:00:31.297
which settles the

00:00:31.109 --> 00:00:32.217
long-open question

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:32.712
of whether it

00:00:32.524 --> 00:00:33.479
could reach 2

00:00:34.014 --> 00:00:35.028
Full story on

00:00:34.839 --> 00:00:35.735
the dashboard
